+# Epic 3 Retrospective: Venue Operations and Public Reliability
+
+Date: 2026-03-19
+Epic: 3
+Project: bmad-6-workshop-migration
+Retrospective Type: Epic completion review
+Previous Retrospective: Epic 2 retrospective found and reviewed
+Epic Update Required: No immediate planning rewrite required
+
+## Epic Summary
+
+Epic 3 completed the operator-facing reliability surface without breaking the public display contract. The repo now supports local-only ops access, shared readiness semantics, degraded-impact diagnostics, lightweight maintenance actions, and restart-aware recovery using the same canonical dashboard model.
+
+- Completed stories: 5/5
+- Sprint status at review: `epic-3: done`
+- Retrospective status: completed by this artifact
+- Story sequence completed:
+  - 3.1 local-only ops access surface
+  - 3.2 public readiness and current ops state
+  - 3.3 degraded-impact diagnostics by signal and scope
+  - 3.4 lightweight refresh and trust-check actions
+  - 3.5 restart and interruption recovery
+
+## Participants
+
+- Workshop, Project Lead
+- Bob, Scrum Master
+- John, Product Manager
+- Amelia, Developer
+- Quinn, QA Engineer
+- Winston, Architect
+
+## Delivery Metrics
+
+- Completion: 5 of 5 stories marked `done`
+- Story tracking discipline: all Epic 3 stories are represented in `docs/sprint-artifacts`
+- Validation evidence: each completed Epic 3 story records successful validation through `npm run validate`
+- Architecture continuity: the epic consistently extended the existing public snapshot, presenter, and dashboard-service seams instead of creating a parallel ops truth model
+- Review closure: story records show repeated review follow-up and fix cycles across readiness, diagnostics, maintenance action, and recovery behavior
+- Artifact hygiene: one stale Story 3.1 status mismatch was detected during this retrospective and corrected so the story artifact matches sprint tracking
+
+## What Went Well
+
+- Epic 3 preserved one canonical model for both public and operator-facing truth. Readiness, degraded impact, maintenance actions, and recovery all stayed grounded in the same snapshot and response metadata.
+- The public and ops route separation held. The team added meaningful operator capabilities without leaking maintenance affordances into the visitor-facing display.
+- Review feedback kept improving the product in the highest-risk areas. Action-result honesty, degraded-scope clarity, carried-forward recovery semantics, and stale-state wording all tightened before the epic closed.
+- Negative-path behavior got first-class treatment. Denied access, degraded inputs, failed refreshes, and cold-start recovery were all tested as product behavior rather than treated as backend edge cases.
+- Epic 2’s contract-first pattern held up well. Focused server helpers, thin routes, and presenter/view-model boundaries scaled cleanly into ops and recovery work.
+
+## Challenges And Growth Areas
+
+- Artifact synchronization still lagged behind implementation in places. The Epic 2 retro called this out, and Epic 3 still left at least one story artifact in a stale `review` state after the epic had effectively closed.
+- The team repeatedly had to guard against overclaiming certainty. The risk shifted from public-display fallback honesty in Epic 2 to ops-readiness, action-result, and restart-recovery honesty in Epic 3, but the core quality problem stayed the same.
+- Ops work increases the temptation to build parallel status systems. Epic 3 avoided that trap, but several stories explicitly had to reinforce the rule to reuse the public snapshot model rather than invent new ops-only semantics.
+- Deployment and stakeholder acceptance remain less evidenced than repository validation. The code and tests are strong, but repo artifacts still do not show a separate formal venue release or sign-off checkpoint.
+
+## Story-Level Patterns
+
+### Common Success Pattern
+
+The strongest Epic 3 pattern was:
+
+1. reuse an existing server truth model
+2. derive calm operator-facing meaning in focused server helpers
+3. render it through a thin ops shell
+4. protect the behavior with negative-path regression coverage
+
+That pattern appears in all five stories and should remain the default for future venue operations work.
+
+### Common Struggle Pattern
+
+The most common struggle was semantic drift under non-happy paths. Review notes across the epic show repeated risk around:
+
+- action outcomes sounding more certain than the data justified
+- degraded diagnostics overstating healthy areas
+- recovery reads appearing fully current after restart
+- story artifacts lagging behind the final implementation state
+
+This was not a tooling failure. It was the natural pressure point of a product whose main promise is calm, truthful public service under imperfect conditions.
+
+## Key Insights
+
+1. The ops surface is successful because it is not a separate product. It works only when it stays downstream of the same public snapshot semantics.
+2. Recovery is a trust problem, not just an availability problem. A usable carried-forward screen after restart is valuable only if it is labeled honestly until fresh live publication resumes.
+3. Local-only maintenance controls can stay lightweight if they reuse the current health pipeline rather than inventing new control paths.
+4. Negative-path coverage is now a durable team strength. Epic 3 treated denied access, degraded signals, failed maintenance actions, and cold starts as core product states.
+5. Process follow-through improved, but artifact-sync discipline still needs to move from intention to habit.
+
+## Previous Retrospective Follow-Through
+
+Epic 2 committed to four concrete improvements. Epic 3 results against those commitments:
+
+1. Boundary-truth checklist in reviews: ✅ Completed
+   - Evidence: Epic 3 stories repeatedly captured review fixes around readiness honesty, degraded-scope clarity, action-result wording, and restart-recovery truthfulness.
+
+2. Artifact sync as part of definition of done: ⏳ In Progress
+   - Evidence: Epic 3 mostly kept sprint and story artifacts aligned, but Story 3.1 still showed `Status: review` until this retrospective corrected it.
+
+3. Reuse one canonical readiness model across public and ops surfaces: ✅ Completed
+   - Evidence: Stories 3.2 through 3.5 explicitly reused the existing dashboard snapshot and response metadata instead of adding an ops-only taxonomy.
+
+4. Make negative-path coverage mandatory for every Epic 3 story: ✅ Completed
+   - Evidence: story records call out denied access, unavailable paths, degraded diagnostics, failed refresh behavior, and cold-start recovery tests across the epic.
+
+## Next Epic Preview
+
+No Epic 4 definition was found in the planning artifacts.
+
+Implication:
+
+- no immediate epic rewrite is required
+- the next planning cycle should start from the now-complete public-plus-ops runtime boundary
+- future roadmap work should decide whether the next priority is deployment hardening, operational observability, or additional venue workflows
+
+## Significant Discoveries
+
+No fundamental architectural change was uncovered during Epic 3. The approved modular-monolith shape, route separation, and canonical snapshot model all proved sound.
+
+The main durable discoveries are:
+
+- the existing dashboard-service and snapshot-store seams are strong enough to support both routine maintenance and restart recovery
+- operator tooling remains safest when it expresses public-service truth rather than backend mechanics
+- artifact-sync and closeout discipline still deserve explicit enforcement because they do not happen automatically
+
+## Action Items
+
+### Process Improvements
+
+1. Add an explicit closeout pass before marking any future epic complete.
+   - Owner: Bob
+   - Deadline: before the next epic retrospective
+   - Success criteria: every story in the epic has final `Status: done`, final file list alignment, and matching sprint status
+
+2. Preserve a retrospective artifact for every completed epic with continuity checks against the prior retro.
+   - Owner: Bob
+   - Deadline: at each future epic boundary
+   - Success criteria: no epic closes without a saved retrospective and prior-action audit
+
+### Technical Improvements
+
+3. Keep all future ops and resilience work on the canonical dashboard snapshot contract.
+   - Owner: Winston and Amelia
+   - Deadline: before implementation of any future ops-facing story
+   - Success criteria: no new ops-only readiness or recovery taxonomy is introduced without an explicit architecture decision
+
+4. Continue mandatory negative-path regression coverage for access, degraded states, action failure, and recovery semantics.
+   - Owner: Quinn and Amelia
+   - Deadline: starting with the next planned implementation story
+   - Success criteria: each future story adds or updates at least one regression test for a non-happy-path behavior
+
+### Team Agreements
+
+- Public truth and operator truth must remain derived from the same underlying snapshot model.
+- Any carried-forward, fallback, or recovery state must narrow confidence immediately and explicitly.
+- Story artifact hygiene is part of done, not post-hoc cleanup.
+
+## Preparation Tasks For Next Planned Work
+
+- Define the next roadmap target before starting new implementation so the now-complete ops and recovery baseline is used intentionally.
+- Decide whether the next workstream is release hardening, venue deployment evidence, or expanded operational capabilities.
+- Carry forward the Epic 3 testing standard so future work keeps denied, degraded, failed, and recovery states under coverage.
+
+## Critical Path
+
+1. Preserve the canonical snapshot model as the only source of truth for any future ops or resilience work.
+   - Owner: Winston
+
+2. Enforce artifact closeout checks before the next epic is marked complete.
+   - Owner: Bob
+
+3. Decide and document the next epic before implementation resumes.
+   - Owner: John and Workshop
+
+## Readiness Assessment
+
+### Testing And Quality
+
+Status: strong
+
+Evidence:
+
+- every Epic 3 story records `npm run validate`
+- negative-path and regression coverage expanded through access control, readiness, diagnostics, actions, and restart recovery
+- review findings were fixed before epic closure rather than deferred
+
+### Deployment And Release
+
+Status: partially evidenced
+
+Evidence:
+
+- repository validation is strong
+- restart and maintenance behavior are represented in automated coverage and story artifacts
+- a separate formal venue deployment acceptance artifact is still not present in the repo
+
+### Stakeholder Acceptance
+
+Status: adequate for epic closeout, but not formally evidenced
+
+Evidence:
+
+- Epic 3 work remains aligned with the approved PRD, UX, and architecture documents
+- no unresolved review findings remain in the Epic 3 story artifacts after closeout
+
+### Technical Health
+
+Status: strong, with one recurring watch area
+
+Healthy areas:
+
+- route separation between public and ops
+- canonical readiness and recovery modeling
+- file-backed restart resilience
+- calm, bounded maintenance action design
+
+Watch area:
+
+- artifact and semantic drift at closeout remain the highest process risk
+
+### Unresolved Blockers
+
+No blockers in the repo artifacts prevent roadmap planning from continuing.
+
+## Commitments And Next Steps
+
+- Use Epic 3 as the stable baseline for any future operations or resilience work.
+- Keep retrospectives and artifact-sync checks mandatory at epic boundaries.
+- Maintain the rule that recovery, degraded state, and maintenance behavior must all stay honest, bounded, and grounded in the public snapshot model.
+- Define the next epic before new implementation begins so this baseline is extended deliberately.
+
+## Closing Summary
+
+Epic 3 was successful because it added real venue-operator capability without corrupting the public display’s calm, truthful contract. The main lesson is that the architecture is sound, but closeout discipline still needs reinforcement. The product is now materially stronger operationally: operators can judge readiness, understand degraded scope, act through a local-only maintenance surface, and confirm restart recovery without exposing internal mechanics to visitors.
